  "id": "bilateral-squat",
  "name": "Bilateral Squat",
  "definition": "A bilateral closed-chain compound squat in which both feet remain planted while the hips and knees flex to a reviewed thigh-parallel bottom position, then the athlete returns to standing through hip extension, knee extension, and ankle plantarflexion. The initial roster is limited to hip-width straight-bar back and canonical clean-grip front squats with no external support, no machine guidance, and no floor reset; clean grip is a figure- and mechanics-derived product setup rather than a controlled study factor. The reviewed back-squat sources do not consistently report a high- versus low-bar site, so the canonical back-squat record does not invent that distinction. Vasti and gluteus maximus are the principal training emphases; rectus femoris, gastrocnemius, and soleus remain meaningful dynamic contributors, while the visible medial-hamstring region controls the hip, pelvis, and knee rather than receiving dynamic squat credit from excitation alone. The trunk, loaded grip, shoulder girdle, pelvis, and feet control the free bar and body segments. Hip hinges, floor pulls, hip thrusts and bridges, unilateral or split-stance squats, lunges, step-ups, leg presses, hack and pendulum squats, belt squats, Smith squats, safety-bar and transformer-bar variants, box squats, heel-elevated squats, partial or full-depth squats, overhead squats, squat jumps, and thrusters remain outside this first contract.",
